Ladybird’s privacy settings let the most recently installed extension
that set a value decide it, and report levelOfControl the way
Firefox’s ExtensionSettingsStore::getLevelOfControl() does
(controlled_by_this_extension, controlled_by_other_extensions, or
controllable_by_this_extension for a later install). Turning
networkPredictionEnabled off stops DNS prefetching and preconnecting,
as Firefox’s ext-privacy.js turns off network.dns.disablePrefetch’s
counterpart, the speculative connection limit, and link prefetching.
Chromium keeps the same precedence in
ExtensionPrefValueMap::GetEffectivePrefValue() and maps the setting
through its NetworkPredictionTransformer. The non-regular-scope error
names Ladybird instead of Firefox’s “Firefox does not support the ...
settings scope.”; no extension compares it. WebKit has no privacy API.
